<article class="env-lemma" id="0603">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0603">Lemma <span data-tag="0603">76.15.1</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $h : Z \to X$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$. Let $Z \subset Z'$ be a first order thickening over $X$. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$Z \subset Z'$ is a universal first order thickening, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for any diagram (<a href="/tag/0602" data-tag="0602">76.15.0.1</a>) with $T'$ a scheme a unique dotted arrow exists making the diagram commute, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for any diagram (<a href="/tag/0602" data-tag="0602">76.15.0.1</a>) with $T'$ an affine scheme a unique dotted arrow exists making the diagram commute. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The implications (1) $\Rightarrow $ (2) $\Rightarrow $ (3) are formal. Assume (3) a assume given an arbitrary diagram (<a href="/tag/0602" data-tag="0602">76.15.0.1</a>). Choose a presentation $T' = U'/R'$, see Spaces, Definition <a href="/tag/0263" data-tag="0263">65.9.3</a>. We may assume that $U' = \coprod U'_ i$ is a disjoint union of affines, so $R' = U' \times _{T'} U' = \coprod _{i, j} U'_ i \times _ T' U'_ j$. For each pair $(i, j)$ choose an affine open covering $U'_ i \times _ T' U'_ j = \bigcup _ k R'_{ijk}$. Denote $U_ i, R_{ijk}$ the fibre products with $T$ over $T'$. Then each $U_ i \subset U'_ i$ and $R_{ijk} \subset R'_{ijk}$ is a first order thickening of affine schemes. Denote $a_ i : U_ i \to Z$, resp. $a_{ijk} : R_{ijk} \to Z$ the composition of $a : T \to Z$ with the morphism $U_ i \to T$, resp. $R_{ijk} \to T$. By (3) applied to $a_ i : U_ i \to Z$ we obtain unique morphisms $a'_ i : U'_ i \to Z'$. By (3) applied to $a_{ijk}$ we see that the two compositions $R'_{ijk} \to R'_ i \to Z'$ and $R'_{ijk} \to R'_ j \to Z'$ are equal. Hence $a' = \coprod a'_ i : U' = \coprod U'_ i \to Z'$ descends to the quotient sheaf $T' = U'/R'$ and we win. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>